      "id": "https://www.bleepingcomputer.com/news/security/binance-says-fake-job-interview-led-to-63-billion-crypto-heist/",
      "url": "https://www.bleepingcomputer.com/news/security/binance-says-fake-job-interview-led-to-63-billion-crypto-heist/",
      "title": "Hackers used employee access and wallet authorization to steal $6.34 billion from Binance",
      "content_html": "Cryptocurrency exchange Binance said attackers spent months compromising employee computers, then used stolen credentials and a wallet authorization mechanism to bypass normal transfer controls and steal about $6.34 billion on Oct. 29. The FBI attributed the operation to North Korean-linked hackers; North Korea denied responsibility.\n\nBinance traced the intrusion to a June infection through a tampered Homebrew installer, followed in September by a fake recruitment process that led another engineer to run a malicious coding assignment. The malware replaced a legitimate development component and evaded endpoint security. By Oct. 20, attackers were using a compromised computer and an internal AI tool to access two wallet engineers’ systems.\n\nOn Oct. 29, attackers used a stolen deployment token to run a script and exploit Ethereum’s EIP-7702 authorization feature. Binance said the wallet’s multiparty-computation design meant no single private key could immediately revoke the authorization, requiring it to develop a special procedure. The exchange said it cleared the delegation at 00:10 UTC on Oct. 30. Binance has rebuilt the affected wallet system and AI tool and commissioned independent cybersecurity experts to review the breach.",
      "date_published": "2026-11-03T20:05:50+00:00",
