      "id": "https://www.archdaily.com/1187930/the-invisible-brief-how-laboratory-needs-shape-research-buildings",
      "url": "https://www.archdaily.com/1187930/the-invisible-brief-how-laboratory-needs-shape-research-buildings",
      "title": "The Invisible Brief: How Laboratory Needs Shape Research Buildings",
      "content_html": "<p>A floor that feels solid may still be moving. Vibrations travel through the <a href=\"https://www.archdaily.com/1042057/unearthing-the-ground-architecture-and-the-politics-of-soil\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">ground</a> and across buildings, often at levels too small for people to notice. Most have little effect on everyday activities, but in some <a href=\"https://www.archdaily.com/tag/laboratories\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">research laboratories</a>, instruments measuring <a href=\"https://www.archdaily.com/1186530/from-paper-to-space-matter-as-sensory-architecture\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">matter</a> at the nanoscale can register movements this small, and even traffic several miles away can interfere with an experiment.</p>",
